{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on 7f8a16f0: head E's three contracts. I accept the first\ntwo with small amendments and change the third. Source edits start\nwhen B has landed, as 176190ba says.\n\n1. Per-act methods: accepted.\n   - `handle.intents.\u003ckind\u003e(signer, asked, signing?)` exists for\n     each non-genesis declared act, as an own key. It derives the\n     expected revisions, shapes and signs. It does not submit.\n     `handle.submit` stays the one way to submit, so a kept packet\n     stays the one way to retry.\n   - `handle.intent` and `handle.submit` do not change.\n   - A caller's `asked.expected` is used as given and is never\n     refreshed. The method still reads the summary once and checks\n     the definition digest.\n   - A failed or incomplete read returns before the signer runs. The\n     method never signs with an empty revision map in its place.\n   - The result is your union: the signed packet with the head `at`,\n     or `read`, `definition-mismatch` or `incomplete`.\n   - The method checks no grant, state or guard, and the docs say so.\n\n2. Shared derivations: accepted.\n   - `formFor(definition, kind, context)` wraps B's bound\n     descriptions and keeps the typed declaration fields. It holds no\n     wording, schema or field-type logic of its own.\n   - `slotsFor(definition, item)` returns the declared `parties`,\n     `values` and `refs`. It adds no label, live value or\n     nullability rule.\n   - `actsOn(definition, item, state)` returns a sorted list of act\n     kinds and nothing else. It lists transitions and item-bound\n     comments, and it drops a kind only when an unconditional\n     top-level state guard on the primary subject excludes the\n     state. Add no certainty flag to a row. The docs call the rows\n     candidates and say that they grant nothing.\n   - Nothing uses `actsOn` to hide an MCP tool or a handle method.\n     The MCP catalogue's names, listing and output stay byte-equal.\n   - Request item 2 says that the spike's `formFor` and the CLI\n     browser helpers' `describe` go. The note names where each went.\n\n3. Rule expressions: one amendment.\n   An author already holds typed references in the act body:\n   `f.\u003cname\u003e`, `on.slot(name)` and `also.\u003calias\u003e.slot(name)`. The\n   tag takes those references in its holes. Do not add `.field` and\n   `.slot` constructors that name the same things a second time by\n   string.\n\n       const e = expression(ctx);\n       rule(\"device-setup\", e`${f.minLevel} \u003c= ${f.maxLevel}`)\n\n   - `expression(ctx)` returns the tag only. The tag uses `ctx` to\n     find each slot's group and emits `fields.\u003cname\u003e` or\n     `subjects.\u003calias\u003e.\u003cgroup\u003e.\u003cslot\u003e` with the profile's quoting.\n   - A hole accepts a field reference or a slot reference. It\n     refuses a string, a literal and every computed operand, at\n     compile time.\n   - The result is one ordinary string for `rule(name, string)`.\n     Hoisting, compile findings and `restricted@1` admission do not\n     change. No operator builder, tree, evaluator or new profile.\n   - TypeScript checks the references. It does not check raw text\n     between them, and the docs say so.\n   - If the type system cannot tell a field reference and a slot\n     reference from the other operands, keep your `.field` and\n     `.slot` constructors, and give the reason in one line of the\n     note. Do not ask again for that choice.\n\nTests: the witness of request item 4 is the bar. The Lending\nscenarios run through `handle.intents` against a real scope, and\nthe Lending MCP catalogue stays byte-equal. Add type-level cases\nonly for the wrong-name errors of item 3 and one case for a read\nfailure that stops before the signer.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}